Answers a natural-language question about the data in this Klaviyo account, using an
LLM over the account's profiles, events, and metrics. Read-only.
IMPORTANT: Results are scoped to the API key's resource scopes. Data the key
cannot read is absent rather than flagged, so a low number may reflect
limited scope rather than low volume. Clearly caveat this to the user.
Only use this tool when:
The question is open-ended or spans multiple resources
You don't know which structured tool would answer it
The structured tools don't support the breakdown you need
For known metrics or resources, prefer the structured tools:
get_campaign_report and get_flow_report for campaign and flow performance,
query_metric_aggregates for event-time aggregations, get_profiles and
get_segments for records. Those are faster and return exact, typed results.
This tool only sees account data, so do not use it for questions about how
Klaviyo works as a product.
Returns one of three things, and callers must handle all three:
An answer with supporting rows.
A clarifying question, when the request is ambiguous. Relay it to the
user or resolve it and call again.
An error.
The response also carries the SQL that produced the result set, so you can
explain how a number was derived. It is K-SQL, Klaviyo's internal dialect
built on MySQL syntax, and may contain dialect-specific constructs (e.g.
automatically added joins) that are expected, not a sign the query is
wrong. It is read-only and not a stable interface: do not parse it, store
it, or build on its syntax.
Can take up to 90 seconds. Do not call in a loop or for quick lookups.
Before calling, resolve every named or referenced
Klaviyo object (flow, campaign, segment, list, form, product, and so on) with that object's own
search or read tools when they are available, and name it in the question by kind, exact name, and
id, for example `flow "Welcome Series" (flow id AbC123)`. Do not ask K-SQL to find, match, or
disambiguate objects by name when you can do so yourself; if a name matches several objects, ask
the user which one before calling.

